[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: One card per grammar element; the declaration in the side pane

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-BOARD-GRAMMARS-VIEW
TITLE: One card per grammar element; the declaration in the side pane
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The Grammars view draws one uniform card per grammar element -- tag, UID
prefix, and field/role/node counts -- and puts the full declaration in the
right-hand pane on selection: every field with its kind, choice words and
required flag, and every relation role the element may write. Where a card
sits is not inferred from the grammar: the tab renders the hand-authored
layout docs/sdoc/board/grammar-groups.json, whose sections are top-level
headings over a rule and whose groups are subtitles over a widget -- "cards",
a centered row of named types, or "grid", a 2x2 matrix with one type per
cell. The Definition grid's axis words are DEC-NODE-FAMILIES' own: normative
or descriptive by row, universal or particular by column. The layout also
carries one-line glosses, one per axis word and one per card title, and its
own $comment names where each of them came from: the four axis glosses are
copied verbatim from NAR-CANON-TYPES-GRID, and a type gloss is the first
sentence of that type's NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-* narrative, so a type with
no narrative carries no gloss.
The tab prints a gloss as given and composes none of them, and both guards
refuse a gloss on a name the layout places nowhere. One "cards" group may
take the "rest", every type the layout places nowhere else, so a type the
grammar grows lands in Unsorted rather than off the tab. The layout is the
operator's exception to REQ-BOARD-GRAMMAR-DRIVEN and is guarded twice:
statically by dev/scripts/grammar-groups-check.py, run as the
strictdoc-board-grammar-groups check in nix flake check over the committed
grammar.sgra and layout -- every type placed exactly once or caught by the
one "rest", nothing stale, no empty or doubly-filled grid cell -- with the
check feeding itself one bad layout per rule so a green cannot be a checker
that always passes; and at runtime by checkGroups() in grammars.js, which
re-runs the same rules on every render and raises a red banner at the top of
the tab for any finding, a missing or unreadable layout included, while still
drawing every type and never hiding one. Data is the snapshot's grammar key
(parsed from grammar.sgra, since the JSON export types every field as
String); an element with zero nodes still gets a card, which is how an empty
vocabulary such as COMMENTARY stays visible. The Board inspector's type name
links here: clicking it switches the view and selects that element's card.
The switcher order is Grammars, Plan, Graph, Perspective.

Two further sections close the right-hand pane, and neither reads the
grammar. They read the snapshot's semantics key, the sdoc-semantics/1 payload
the engine computes: Rules, the sentences that engine states about this
type's state fields, then Lifecycle, one machine per state field drawn as an
inline SVG by assets/fsm.js. The grammar says a type has a STATUS field with
four words in it; the semantics engine says which of those words a node may
move to next and under what rule. Rules come first because an operator
shaping the model reads the sentence before the diagram. fsm.js knows no
field name -- DEPTH, STATUS and AUTHORED_BY arrive as data, so a field rename
does not reach it -- and it lays its machines out transposed, rank running
down and branches spreading across, because the pane is 350px wide and a
six-rung ladder never fit it sideways. Its geometry is measured in CSS pixels
with no viewBox, so no label is scaled below the app's 10px floor. Each
section degrades to a named absence: no engine, no machine for this type, or
a machine with no rules yet all still render a section that says so, because
a missing section reads as settled and nothing here is.
<<<
